e top 100 to 200 secretors  this slide just shows a quick summary of  the three different applications as i  mentioned with hybridoma screening  you challenge a mouse with an antigen  you harvest  the b cells from the spleen fuse them  with myeloma cells  and this is what would go into the  screening instrument  so this is the cells would be combined  with the detection reagents  and these detection reagents and the the  sample would then be put into the  uh hypercell instrument or something  like that you'd screen 0.1 to 10 million  cells collect the top 100 to 200  secretors  and then after sample recovery you would  have your positive  hybridoma clones that are you know for  the desired monoclonal antibody  production the second application would  be a direct b cell screening  and this is where the b cells are  combined with the reagents they go into  the reagent prep and into the instrument  and in this case you have positive b  cell clones that are  isolated in the instrument and then the  third application would be that for that  step of  cell line development you know after you  transfect the cell for example  you want to determine whether that cell  has been transfected properly and is  secreting the  the uh protein or antibody of interest  so this type of  single cell screening can be used for  cell line development as well this is  where the cells of interest are put into  the system and out you get  the um the positive cell clones with the  with the correct targets  so uh let's go let's back up for a  second and since this is an educational  talk it's worth  worth the time to talk a little bit  about the concept of partitioning single  cells  so the concept of that quote unquote  digital assays or single cell assays  happens when you have more partitions  than you have cells  so imagine that you have a bunch of  cells in a test tube and those cells are  evenly dispersed  you create a bunch of very very small  partitions  and the cells are evenly distributed  throughout those partitions  so statistically random spacing between  the cells  okay if the number of partitions exceeds  the number of cells  then this lambda value that you see here  at the top is going to be less than one  and that's when you're in well within  the poisson regime  where many of the partitions are going  to be empty shown in red  and some of them will have one or more  cells  if you choose a lambda of 0.1 the  majority 90  will be empty and about 10 will have a  single cell  very very few will have more than one  cell if you choose lambda equals one  you can see in this graph here that the  um the distribution changes a little bit  it'll be just  under forty percent for zero and one  cells and then  um you know there's also the possibility  of having two three four  uh cells in the partitions and and uh so  on with lambda equals three  um one of the key messages here is  having more partitions  allows you to analyze more cells okay  in order to keep the lambda low if you  want to screen a number of a lot of  cells you have to have a lot of  partitions  so there's an advantage to having more  partitions and a few examples of  where many partitions it has helped  in the so-called digital assays which  are not only limited to cells but you  can think about things like digital pcr  which is for quantifying nucleic acids  digital elisa  for quantifying proteins  single cell transcriptomics like  techniques like drop seek  where you you perform single cell  screening um  single cell sequencing i'm sorry and  then the one that we're going to be  talking about today is single cell  secretion assays  so first i just want to mention quickly  about the concept of polydispersity  this question often comes up is that  well if your partitions are not  equal to each other if they don't have  the same volume how do you handle  um you know how do you perform any type  of quantitation  and without getting into all the details  of this i just want to highlight the  main point here  is that if one can  measure the volume of every single pod  every single partition  then the accuracy of quantitation  is actually the same as a mono-dispersed  population  and so this slide shows some monte carlo  simulations that we've done  of that we've compared our method which  is in green  to the blue line here which represents  monodispersed partitions i'm talking  specifically about  the charts on the on the upper right  side here  so if you have a lambda on the x-axis  and your measur  and your your measured lambda on the  y-axis you would like to see  a a line with a slope of one and so you  can see that the bioelectronic  polydisperse model basically follows  that line so it's  as as accurate as having monitors first  partitions provided that you can measure  the volume of each partition  so this is where computer vision comes  in because we can with computer vision  we can actually  use a camera and algorithms to actually  measure the volume of every single  pod that comes through  all right so this slide shows  a variety of single cell assays and  i've shown this because you know as the  educational portion of the talk  i think the audience will find  interesting is that there are many many  different types of single cell assets  that have been developed  this is just a sampling of them that is  actually from  2017 a review paper i wrote at that time  there are many many more even now at  this point um  you know four years later so um  just as to establish some kind of basis  uh i'll give you some examples um you  can look at intracellular gene  expression part a here  this is where a single cell is  expressing an enzyme in the in the  intracellular environment you can use  fluorogenic substrates to measure the  activity of the enzyme  b suppose the cell secretes the enzyme  into the partition around it again you  can use a fluorogenic substrate to  quantify  the amount or activity of the secreted  enzyme  part c is where you have an enzyme label  that's attached to cell surface  biomarkers  again with the fluorogenic substrate  this technique would be used to quantify  cell surface biomarkers  part d is where you have service display  enzymes again with fluorogenic  substrates to quantify the amount  and activity of the enzyme part e  is where you're using um fret assay  with or i'm sorry fluorogenic substrate  assay to measure  the um uh the metabolism of the cell  using oxidase and peroxidase enzymes you  can look at single cell metabolism  you can look at live dead aces for  cytotoxicity screening part  f by staining the cells within  the partition either you know green or  red depending on live or dead  part g as many of you heard about it  drop seek or single cell transcriptomics  is where a cell is co-encapsulated with  dna barcoded  mrna capture beads so when the cell is  lice the mrna transcripts  bind to the bead and the bead actually  has a specific  barcode that links this drop  i'm sorry that links the bead to the  cell  after this is this process is done you  can pull all the partitions together  you can run them through a single  sequencing run and so the dna barcode of  beads will  allow you to see the transcripts of  thousands of cells at any given time  part h is where you can use a single  cell screening techniques for looking at  the quantity of bacteria  okay so let's talk a little bit about  bioelectronica single cell secretion  assays  i mentioned that we are using a  non-fluorescent mechanism  so let's just talk about that a little  bit we have a proprietary bead-based  reagent chemistry  which clusters in the presence of a  particular  antibody so a secreting a pod that  contains a secreting cell a hit  would have a cluster of beads whereas  empty pods or non-secreting cells  would have the beads dispersed evenly  throughout the pods  so then we would collect the hits and we  would  you know not collect the empty pods or  non-secreting cells  so this is a non-fluorescent clustering  mechanism that's compatible with  lensless computer vision  and i'll talk about that in just a  second so the advantages of having a  non-fluorescent assay you avoid things  like photobleaching you avoid  a certain type of reagents that could be  toxic to cells or  reagents which are expensive to develop  uh bead-based assay chemistries are you  know they've  um they're a fairly robust method of  performing assays  uh they're also compatible with  bioelectronic cell growth media  the detection reagents can be customized  for hybridomas and b  cells that are based on a specific  epitope  and they can also be used to detect  activated t cells based on the  specific kind of secretion  so on the left here you'll see an  immunoglobulin assay a negative control  and a positive control  in this case all the pods have um  a physiologically relevant level of igg  in them in the positive control  negative control of course has no igg in  the negative control you can see the  beads dispersed evenly throughout the  pod  as i mentioned in the positive control  you can see clusters being formed  you can take this one step further to a  single cell secretion assay  so this is where single cells are  encapsulated you know in the poisson  regime like i mentioned earlier  pre-incubation you can see the beads are  evenly dispersed throughout  so this is where the cells have not had  enough time to  obtain a sufficient concentration of  antibody  to secrete a sufficient amount of  antibody into the pods  for them to be detected post incubation  you can clearly see the difference  with the red circles that these uh  certain  pods have single cells in them and those  cells are  creating these clusters through their  antibody secretions  so the hypercell system takes this  bead-based chemistry  and it performs a sorting essentially so  you have the secreting cells which  secreting the pods that contain  secreting cells which are considered a  hit  you have the empty pods and the  non-secreting cells  secreting cells are are directed to the  hip  collect outlet using a proprietary  electrofluidic sorter  with integrated electrodes that are  triggered by computer vision  and the empty pods and non-secreting  cells go into the waste  out collect passively so some of the  highlights of the hypercell system is  that it performs gentle sorting with low  shear rates and this can  help with cell viability i'll talk about  why that's the case  and so it would have the ability to sort  hybridomas cho cells b cells and t  cells and so on  so let's talk a little bit about  lensless imaging this is one of the  interesting aspects of the  bioelectronica system  lensless imaging is where the pods  are flown directly over the cmos imager  and you can see that the reason we call  them pods the o  and the pod stand for oblate meaning  they're not spherical they're squashed  and so what we do is we squash them and  then we flow them over the cmos imager  and it turns out that squashing helps  you  to be able to see the clusters better  so this approach you'll notice it has no  microscope objectives the imager is  directly underneath where the pods are  flowing  this approach offers high resolution so  it offers micron scale resolution over a  wide field of view  it is robust in the sense that it  alleviates a planarity and optical  alignment issues  and it's most importantly that it's  scalable  so you know by not having microscope  objectives and using cmos imagers that  are relatively well  available at this point  we are able to have multiple detectors  multiple sorting elements that are  operating in parallel so  the concept of hypercell is throughput  by parallelization  i'll show you an image here here we have  four  channels in parallel that are all imaged  using just a single cmos imager  uh so essentially it's like having four  flow cytometers  in parallel now you can see in one of  these regions here  a hit is detected by our computer vision  system  a single camera can accommodate four  detection windows and four parallel  channels  and the hyper cell system currently has  four cameras  so that's a total of 16 channels that  are operating  in parallel and  the advantages of doing that is that you  don't have a really high flow rate in  any particular channel the flow rate is  actually  distributed among the channels and so  that gives you lower shear stresses  to improve cell viability and this is  all because the lensless imaging  is a scalable approach where you can you  can replicate or array  your detection windows  uh this slide talks a little bit about  quantifying with computer vision so the  next question is yeah now that we've  we can perform imaging how do we  actually quantify well  we use our computer vision system we've  developed specific algorithms  for identifying both the size of the pod  as well as identifying the contents of  the pod  and both is important because you can  think about normalization type  procedures right  small pods obviously won't have many  beads in them so the clusters in them  would expect be expected to be smaller  so by by using like various types of  normalization  statistical methods we can accommodate  the uh poly dispersed population  and have similar results to a  monodispersed one  so this is an example of low  concentration at the top and high  concentration  at the bottom and you can clearly see  the differences between the two one of  the rules of computer vision is that  if you can visually see the differences  between  two types of populations and computer  vision  and various types of statistical or  machine learning methods can be used to  tell the difference between them  just so just to highlight again this is  what a typical uh  you know a hit might look like  uh you can see that within this  microscope image you can see this the  signal from the secreted antibody which  is a cluster of beads  and notice that the beads are actually  attached to  the the cell itself this is this can  often be the case of the cell if some of  the  biomarkers are being secreted to the  external environment and some of them  are actually  still on the cell membrane this can also  be  detected in our computer vision system  the pentagram here is our detection  window  where the computer vision is applied and  uh based on what  the computer vision sees within this  pentagram a sorting decision is made and  it gets directed to the hit collect  outlet or the waste collect using our  you know proprietary electrofluidic  sorting system  uh this technology we're quite happy  that  last year 2020 the society of laboratory  automation and screening  awarded hypercell the best new  technology product award  and then finally there's also the  question of you know how much throughput  can you really get  this slide sort of alludes to that here  we use rapid computer vision algorithm  you'll see this  oh i'm sorry like let me let me just  save the video i'll play it again  okay so in this video you can see what's  happening  is that the computer vision algorithms  are analyzing  not only the pods the volume and  velocity and the shape of the pods but  also what is inside the pods  the size and the darkness of the  clusters that are formed  and there are certain methods that we  can use to determine cell counts as well  so um the question then becomes about  you know  how fast can computer vision analyze  we've we've shown that by  optimizing your algorithms by using  parallelization methods  we can analyze more than 500 000 pods  in in less than 15 minutes which comes  out to throughputs of  um you know more than a thousand pots  per second so  you know traditional flow cytometers are  working actually in the same range  the difference is is that a single  channel flow cytometer is all going  through a single channel so the detector  has to operate very rapidly  but if you have multiple detectors  operating parallel they don't have to  have the same throughput requirements  number one and number two again as i  mentioned earlier  there there's less shear stress when you  have multiple channels operating in  parallel  now the computer vision itself extracts  multiple parameters up to 20 different  parameters using computer vision  and those parameters offer a great deal  of flexibility  in choosing um in in basically defining  the thresholds for your sorting  um so at bioelectronica we like to say  that there's  that we can software define a new  a new assay so by choosing you know some  bead-based reagents and software  defining our thresholds  you can quickly adapt to different cell  types different antibody types and so on  so just to summarize some of the key  innovations here  with the hypercell system so the  hypercell uses novel bead-based  detection reagents which are adaptable  to a variety of  polyclonal monoclonal antibodies they're  compatible with lensless imaging and  don't require fluorescence  so we've shown that we can use computer  vision to trigger the sorting  of single cells based on their secretion  profiles in concert with  the bead-based detection reagents so  this provides like  softer programmable sorting thresholds  we use electrofluidic chips from the pcb  industry the electronic supply chain  which allows us to integrate flow  control sorting and control electronics  and we use poly disperse rather than  monitor mono disperse  dispersions this allows for high  throughputs and allows users  to use standard laboratory vortexers  instead of the  the microfluidic droplet generators  and then finally and most importantly is  this concept of throughput by  parallelization all these components are  designed with  with this concept in mind so that we can  have multiple detection windows multiple  sorting elements operating in parallel  so that each sorting element doesn't  experience  too much flow rate or too much shear  stress which would  impact cell viability so this approach  helps us to improve cell  viability which is which is great for  when you're working with sensitive cell  types for example like d cells  so to summarize some of the learning  objectives again i hope uh hope at this  point that you've  understood a little bit more about the  role of single cell screening in  antibody discovery  some of the concepts of single cell  partitioning some of the methods that  have been used for single cell screening  and how  the hyper cell system compares to those  and and just some of the concepts that  are used in our system so clustering  lensless imaging computer vision working  together  to perform uh single cell antibody  secretions  based sorting so with that i'd like to  thank you  uh for your attention and interest in  the hypercell system and i'd also like  to thank the many members of the  bioelectronica team  just a small sampling are shown in this  picture bioelectronic is  based out of reno nevada which is  actually quite a beautiful place  if you've never had a chance to visit we  welcome you  so thanks again and thanks again to lab  roots for the opportunity  to present to you today
 

